---
import Base   from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav    from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';

// [CONFIRM: exact day of the last revision, to match Heart's '5 May 2026']
const lastUpdated = 'May 2026';
const contactEmail = 'mail.roshankumargupta@gmail.com';

const sections = [
  {
    n: '1',
    title: 'Who Operates Whispr',
    body: `Whispr is written and maintained by one developer in Würzburg, Germany, publishing under the name PointBreakLab (pointbreaklab.com). The operator is the data controller for the purposes of applicable privacy law (GDPR / DSGVO).\n\nContact: ${contactEmail}`,
  },
  {
    n: '2',
    title: 'What Data We Collect',
    body: `We collect nothing.\n\nWhispr has no registration step and no backend to register against. The build carries no analytics SDK, and the app never opens a connection to a PointBreakLab server.`,
  },
  {
    n: '3',
    // Mesh relay measured by the owner, n=2: under 5 km in ~1 h and 1 h 25 min.
    // The HOP COUNT on those runs is still unmeasured.
    title: 'How a Message Travels',
    body: `Messages travel directly between devices over three channels:\n\n• Local Wi-Fi (LAN), direct HTTP between devices on the same network, with every message wrapped in ECIES application-layer encryption so contents are invisible to everything else on that network.\n\n• Bluetooth LE mesh, multi-hop relay between nearby devices for offline messaging. Payloads are end-to-end encrypted before entering the mesh. This is the least dependable of the three: through walls, with people moving, a hop can simply fail.\n\n• Tor hidden services, over the internet via the Tor anonymity network. Your IP address is not revealed to your contact.\n\nAll three channels apply end-to-end encryption using the Signal Protocol (X3DH key agreement + Double Ratchet algorithm) before any data leaves your device. PointBreakLab cannot decrypt your messages even if compelled by law.`,
  },
  {
    n: '4',
    title: 'Local Data Storage',
    body: `All app data is stored exclusively on your device:\n\n• Messages, contacts, and settings are stored in an SQLite database encrypted with SQLCipher (AES-256-CBC full-file encryption) on Android and iOS.\n\n• Cryptographic private keys are stored in your device's secure hardware enclave: Android Keystore on Android, iOS Keychain on iPhone.\n\n• The database encryption key is derived from your password using PBKDF2-SHA512 (200,000 iterations) and is never stored in plaintext.\n\n• Whispr does not use iCloud, Google Drive, or any cloud backup for message data. If you use the built-in encrypted backup feature, the backup file is saved to your local device storage, and you pick the folder.`,
  },
  {
    n: '5',
    // OWNER DECISION: first person "I" proposed here. The Firebase admission is the most
    // human passage on the page and would land harder as "I could not find a QR scanner
    // that does not drag Firebase in with it." Not shipped: site-wide person is unresolved.
    // [CONFIRM: which QR libraries were tried before ML Kit]
    title: 'Third-Party Services',
    body: `Whispr carries no ad SDK and no social-login button.\n\nThe only third-party component that may contact an external server is the embedded Tor daemon, which connects to the Tor network for routing, not to any PointBreakLab server. The Tor Project's privacy policy applies to that connection (torproject.org).\n\nThe QR-code scanning library (Google ML Kit via mobile_scanner) includes Firebase Transport Runtime as a transitive dependency. This component is bundled but does not transmit data; it is used only for local barcode detection. No Firebase project is configured in Whispr. The runtime is there because it arrives with ML Kit.`,
  },
  {
    n: '6',
    title: 'Device Permissions',
    body: `The battery permission is the one worth arguing about: Android throttles the Tor service the moment the screen goes off, and Whispr has no server to hold a message in the meantime.`,
    table: [
      ['Bluetooth',     'Discover nearby Whispr contacts and exchange messages over BLE mesh without internet.'],
      ['Camera',        'Scan QR codes to add contacts or restore your account. No photos are taken or stored.'],
      ['Microphone',    'Record encrypted voice messages. Audio is encrypted locally before sending and never transmitted to us.'],
      ['Local Network', 'Discover Whispr peers on the same Wi-Fi network for direct LAN messaging.'],
      ['Notifications', 'Display incoming message alerts locally. No notification content passes through any external server.'],
      ['Battery opt.',  'Prevent Android from throttling the Tor service when the screen is off.'],
    ],
  },
  {
    n: '7',
    title: 'Data Retention & Deletion',
    body: `Everything lives on your device, so you delete it yourself:\n\n• Uninstalling the app removes all stored data.\n\n• The "Data Burn" feature (Settings → Security → Panic Button) performs a 3-pass cryptographic wipe: the database encryption key is destroyed first (making all stored data permanently unreadable), all table rows are deleted, and the database file is overwritten three times before unlinking. On flash storage the overwrite is the weakest of those three steps; destroying the key is what actually makes the data unreadable.\n\n• Disappearing messages are automatically deleted from your device after the timer you set expires.\n\nThere is no data stored with PointBreakLab to delete.`,
  },
  {
    n: '8',
    title: "Children's Privacy",
    body: `Whispr is not directed at children under 13 (or under 16 in the European Economic Area). We do not knowingly collect information from children. Because Whispr collects no data from any user, no special handling of children's data is required.`,
  },
  {
    n: '9',
    title: 'Your Rights (GDPR / CCPA)',
    body: `Under GDPR, CCPA, and similar laws you have rights including access, rectification, erasure, and portability of your personal data.\n\nPointBreakLab holds no personal data about you, so there is nothing on this end to access, correct, export, or delete, and everything those rights cover is already sitting in the encrypted database on your phone where you can reach it without asking anyone.`,
  },
  {
    n: '10',
    title: 'Security',
    body: `• End-to-end encryption: X3DH + Double Ratchet (Signal Protocol), AES-256-GCM per message.\n\n• At-rest encryption: SQLCipher AES-256 full-database encryption on mobile.\n\n• Key protection: private keys stored in Android Keystore / iOS Keychain with hardware-backed protection.\n\n• Transport: ECIES application-layer encryption on LAN; Tor hidden services for internet transport; BLE mesh, multi-hop, for offline relay.\n\nNo security system is perfect. Whispr is provided as-is without warranty. See the in-app Security Guide for best practices.`,
  },
  {
    n: '11',
    // [CONFIRM: how many revisions since v1.0]
    title: 'Changes to This Policy',
    body: `This document changes only when a new build of the app ships, because nothing can push it out on its own. The copy inside Whispr and the copy at pointbreaklab.com are edited together. The date at the top of this document reflects the most recent revision.`,
  },
  {
    n: '12',
    title: 'Contact',
    body: `Questions about this Privacy Policy:\n\nPointBreakLab\n${contactEmail}\npointbreaklab.com`,
  },
];

const dataTable = [
  ['Messages',       'Encrypted on your device with AES-256-GCM, and never sent anywhere.'],
  ['Contacts',       'In the same encrypted database as the messages, and no copy of it is uploaded.'],
  ['Identity key',   'Generated on your device, stored in your device keychain. We never see it.'],
  ['Display name',   'Stored locally. Shared only with contacts you explicitly message.'],
  ['IP address',     'Used only while finding peers on your own Wi-Fi. Never logged and never sent to us.'],
  ['Crash + analytics', 'No crash-reporter SDK and no analytics SDK are in the build, so neither one runs.'],
  ['Advertising ID', 'Never accessed.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="Whispr Privacy Policy"
  description="Whispr has no backend. Messages, contacts and identity keys stay in an encrypted database on your phone, and the app never connects to PointBreakLab."
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="pt-32 pb-24 px-6">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

      <!-- Header -->
      <header class="section-reveal mb-12 text-center">
        <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-accent/10 border border-accent/30 text-accent text-xs font-mono tracking-wider mb-5">
          Last updated: {lastUpdated}
        </span>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary mb-5">Whispr Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mx-auto">
          There is no Whispr server. Messages go device to device over Wi-Fi, Bluetooth or Tor, and the keys that would decrypt them never leave your phone.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Highlight banner -->
      <div class="section-reveal flex items-start gap-3 p-5 rounded-2xl bg-accent/5 border border-accent/30 mb-16">
        <svg class="shrink-0 mt-0.5 text-accent" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 22 22" fill="none">
          <path d="M11 2L4 5v6c0 4 3 7.5 7 9 4-1.5 7-5 7-9V5l-7-3z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          <path d="M8 11l2 2 4-4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
        <p class="text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
          The one component that reaches the internet on its own is the bundled Tor daemon, and it reaches the Tor network, never a PointBreakLab address.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Sections -->
      {sections.map(({ n, title, body, table }) => (
        <section class="section-reveal mb-12">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 mb-4">
            <span class="shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-accent text-bg flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold font-mono">{n}</span>
            <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-bold text-primary">{title}</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="text-secondary text-sm md:text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-line pl-10">
            {body}
          </div>

          {n === '2' && (
            <div class="pl-10 mt-5">
              <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface overflow-hidden text-sm">
                {dataTable.map(([k, v], i) => (
                  <div class={`flex gap-4 px-4 py-3 ${i < dataTable.length - 1 ? 'border-b border-border' : ''}`}>
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-32 text-hint font-mono text-xs">{k}</span>
                    <span class="text-secondary text-xs md:text-sm leading-relaxed">{v}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
          )}

          {table && (
            <div class="pl-10 mt-5">
              <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface overflow-hidden text-sm">
                {table.map(([k, v], i) => (
                  <div class={`flex gap-4 px-4 py-3 ${i < table.length - 1 ? 'border-b border-border' : ''}`}>
                    <span class="shrink-0 w-32 text-hint font-mono text-xs">{k}</span>
                    <span class="text-secondary text-xs md:text-sm leading-relaxed">{v}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
          )}
        </section>
      ))}

      <!-- Footer note -->
      <div class="section-reveal text-center pt-10 border-t border-border">
        <p class="text-hint text-xs">© 2024–2026 PointBreakLab. All rights reserved.</p>
      </div>

    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
